			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-20T02:16:37Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				356,
				2
			],
			"Message": "feat(Common): Add editor Sky events and call/type hierarchy prep\n\nExtend the IPC surface in SkyEvent.rs with five new editor-related event URIs used by Mountain→Sky communication for text editor interaction:\n\n- DecorationSetRanges (\"sky://decoration/set-ranges\") — fired when an extension calls setDecorations, enabling Monaco decoration rendering via ICodeEditorService\n- EditorApplyTextEdits (\"sky://editor/apply-text-edits\") — payload channel for editor.edit() calls with range+text edits\n- EditorSelectionChanged (\"sky://editor/selection-changed\") — forwards selection changes from Sky to Cocoon via window.didChangeTextEditorSelection\n- EditorActiveChanged (\"sky://editor/active-changed\") — fires when a text editor tab is activated, passing uri, viewColumn, selections\n- EditorRevealRange (\"sky://editor/revealRange\") — scrolls Monaco to a specific range with a reveal type\n\nAdd PrepareCallHierarchy and PrepareTypeHierarchy as required entry-point methods on the LanguageFeatureProviderRegistry trait. These sit alongside the existing ProvideTypeHierarchySupertypes/Subtypes methods, completing the hierarchy provider lifecycle — Mountain must call Prepare before requesting incoming/outgoing calls, matching the VS Code LanguageFeatureRequest pattern.\n\nBoth additions close gaps identified during Wind/Cocoon integration of editor decorations and call/type hierarchy features.",
			"SHA": "7b86b6829d1cd1082e22bdd79392125bb29890c1"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-20T00:52:27Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				356,
				1
			],
			"Message": "refactor(Common): Silently handle access failures in Access function\n\nRemove the explicit error logging and unused catch variable from the `Access` function in `TypeScript/Function/Access.ts`. Replace the `console.log` statement with a silent catch block.\n\nAccess failures within this utility are considered non-critical by design. Swallowing the error prevents noise in the development console and avoids halting execution when optional lookups fail. This aligns with the existing pattern of graceful degradation for non-blocking operations.",
			"SHA": "2c9f2e76ccf403293103edd9403cde908a2aac10"
		}
	],
